Macedonians Vote in Referendum on Whether to Change Their Country's Name
The citizens of Macedonia voted in a televised referendum this Sunday whether the name of the state should be changed to the "Republic of North of Macedonia". It is a move that would have been resolving a decades-long dispute with Greece, which has blocked Macedonia's membership in the European Union and NATO.
Greece, where there is an area called Macedonia, argues that its northern neighbor's name poses a pretensions to its territorial integrity, thus vetoing its inclusion in NATO and the EU.
The two governments reached an agreement in June about the proposed new name, but nationalist opponents say the change would undermine the ethnic identity of Macedonia's Slavic majority.
President Georghe Ivanov said that he himself will not vote in the referendum, while at the same time a campaign to boycott him casts a shadow of doubt on whether the referendum will be able to reach the minimum 50 per cent, which is required to recognise its validity.
"Are you supporting membership in NATO and the EU by adopting the agreement with Greece?"
Proponents of the name change, which include Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that the price is worth paying in order to achieve the entry of the state of Macedonia formed after the disintegration of Yugoslavia into organizations such as the EU and NATO.
"Today I came to give my vote for the future of the country, for the young Macedonians, so that they can live freely under the umbrella of the European Union, as this means a safer life for all of us," Olyviera Georgievska, a 79-year-old citizen of Skopje.
Although the referendum does not have binding force, enough members of the parliament have ruled that they will comply with its outcome to such an extent that the vote already has a decisive burden.
In order to change the name of the state, two-thirds of the parliamentary representatives have to vote.
The State Electoral Commission said there had been no reports of irregularities until 1 afternoon.
However, the turnout was just 16 per cent, up from 34 per cent in the last general election in 2016, when 66 per cent of registered voters visited the polls.
"I have come to vote because of my children, our place is in Europe," said Gyose Tanevski, a 62-year-old from the capital Skopje.
Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zoritsa and their son Dusko voted in the referendum in Macedonia to change the name of the country, which will open the way for accession to NATO and the European Union, in Strumica on September 30, 2018.
In front of the parliament in Skopje Vladimir Kavadarkov at the age of 54 was preparing a small stage and arranging chairs in front of the tents stretched by the boycotting referendum.
"We support NATO and the EU, but we want to join them with our heads proudly raised, and not through the mination door," Kavadarkov said.
“Our country may be poor, but we still have dignity.
“If they don’t want to accept us as Macedonia, we can turn to others like China and Russia and become part of the Euro-Asian alliance.”
Prime Minister Zaev argues that membership in NATO will lead to the investments needed for Macedonia, as unemployment in the country is more than 20 percent.
"I believe that the vast majority will be with us, as more than 80 per cent of our citizens prefer the EU and NATO," Zaev said after casting his own vote.
The stateswoman also stated that the positive result will lead to "affirmation of the future of our people."
Last Monday, Macedonia's Institute for Political Studies published the results of a survey that between 30 and 43 per cent of voters will take part in the referendum - which is below the required turnout.
Another poll conducted by Macedonian broadcaster Thelma reported that 57 per cent of respondents planned to vote on Sunday.
Of those, 70 per cent have said they're going to vote "Yes."
In order for the referendum to be successful, the turnout must be 50% plus one vote.
If the referendum is unsuccessful, it will represent the first serious blow against pro-Western government's policies since winning power in May last year.
