"Sea Is Ourss": Unleaded to the Sea of Bollivia Deployes the Judgment Reiterates Road to the Pacific
Sailors are constantly enviscateurizing with rigging naval headquarters in La Paz.
An ocean blue flap flies over public buildings.
Marine bases from Lake Titikaka to the Amazon are adorned with the motto: "The sea belongs to us in law.
“It’s our duty to get it back.”
Across Bollivia, which has no access to the ocean, the remembrance of the lost outlet conveyed to Chile after a bloody conflict in the 19th-in-a-century is still still alive - as is the call to be sailed through the Pacific again.
In fact, this hope at the moment is at the strongest in decades, while Bolivia is waiting for an adjournment by the international court on October 1, having already passed five years of consideration.
"The power is in Bolivia, there is resilience and unity everywhere, as the country expects a positive result," Bolivian diplomat Roberto Calzadilla said.
Many Bolivians will be looking at the ruling of the international court on the big screen across the country, hoping that the Hague tribunal will support Bolivia's claim and that after decades of negotiations Chile would be required to give Bolivia sovereign access to the ocean.
Evo Morales, Bolivia's charismatic local president who is about to head to a contested re-deliberation battle next year, has also pawned a lot and a lot on the court's judgment on Monday.
“We’re very close to regaining access to the Pacific,” Moralez promised last August.
"But some analyst analysts believe that the likelihood that the court will make a decision in favor of Bolivia is quite small. Moreover, even if it rules in its favor, it would not make a difference in reality.
The U.N. body in the Netherlands does not have the power to seize the territory of Chile and has decreed that it will not determine the outcome of possible negotiations.
The fact that the judgment of the international court is only six months after hearing the latest arguments points out that the case is “not complicated,” Paz Zaratte, a Chilean expert on international law, retorts.
And instead of contributing to Bolivia's quest, the last four years they may actually have harmed it.
"The issue of access to the ocean was bloated by Bolivia's current government," Zaraate argues.
He then implied that Moralez's belligerent rhesium had erased Chili's remaining good-hength.
Bollivia and Chile at some point will continue the negotiations, but it will be extremely difficult to have a normal dialogue after what happened.
The two countries have not maintained diplomatic missions to each other since 1962.
Previous President Eduardo Rodriguez Veltze, Bolivia's representative in The Hague, dismissed the notion that the court's decision was being taken unusually quickly.
According to him on Monday, Bollivia will have an "incredible opportunity to reopen the page of a new era in its relationship with Chile," as well as a chance to "felt 139 years of discontinuity by achieving a benefit for both sides."
Calzadilla also dismissed the notion that Morales – who continues to be one of the most popular presidents in Latin America – has exploited the problem of ocean access as a “policy crutch.”
“Bolivia will never relinquish her right to go to the Pacific,” he added.
Judgment is an opportunity to be seen that we have to overcome our past problems.”
