John Edward: Language Skills Are Essential for Global Entities
Independent schools in Scotland have maintained a reputation for academic excellence, with this continuing in 2018 with another superstudied performance of exam scores, which has only been buoyed by individual and collegiative successes in sports disciplines, art, music and other social sciences.
With up to 30,000 students all over Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Association of Independent Schools (SCIS), are looking to offer the best level to their pupils and their parents.
Indigenous schools aim to prepare their students for their further education, their chosen quarries and their place in a global world.
As an e-learning sector that can compose and put into action a personal curriculum, we see that modern languages continue to be a popular and desirable subject in schools.
Nelson Mandela once said, “If you’re talking to someone in a language they understand, your words get to them.
“But if you speak his own language, you are touching his heart.”
“It’s a mighty reminder that we can’t just rely on the English language when we want to build relationships and trust with the peoples of other states.
Through the results of this year's exams, we're seeing that the languages come first with the highest-performing for independent-magnificent schools.
A total of 68 per cent of students who have studied foreign languages have achieved the high A rating.
Figures, which were compiled by 74 SCIS member schools, showed that 72 per cent of students achieved an A-Chin-Chinese estimate with 72 per 2 per cent of learners, 69 per cent of French learners and 63 per 3 per cent of those with Spanish.
This shows that independent schools in Scotland perceive foreign languages as vital skills that children and youngsters will definitely need in the future.
At the moment, languages as an object of free choice by students receive the same attitude as natural-mathematical disciplines (scientific, technological, engineering and mathematical disciplines) both in independent schools and beyond.
Cariled in 2014 by the Commission on Employment and Occupational Skills of the United Kingdom, a study has been shown that from the reasons why employers have struggled to find employees, 17 per cent have indicated insufficient language competencies.
Because of this, the knowledge of foreign languages has become mandatory in order to prepare youngsters for their future careers.
More and more working positions require the mastery of a foreign language, which makes these skills essential in a globalized world.
Irrespective of the chosen quarry, if a person masters a foreign language, it will have a real advantage in the future, having possesses a similar skill that remains for a lifetime.
Opportunity to socialize directly with people from other countries automatically puts the proficiency of a few languages a person in front of the competition.
According to a YouGov survey of more than 4,000 UK adults in 2013, 75 per cent were unfamiliar with a foreign language well enough to be able to conduct a language, with French being the only language whose proficiency reached a double-digit percentage with an indicator of 15 per cent.
That's why investing in language learning today is important for children now.
Producing several languages, especially those of developing economies, will provide children with a better chance of finding a good job.
In Scots, every school will be distinguished from languages taught.
A number of schools are going to focus on more classic modern languages, while others will teach languages that are seen as more important for the United Kingdom in a future plan by 2020, such as Chinese or Japanese.
No matter which interests your child has, there will always be enough languages from which to choose in independent schools, with educators being specialists in their field.
Investigative schools in Scotland are vehement to provide an educational environment that will prepare children and give them the skills they need to succeed in life, no matter what the future entails.
At the moment it cannot be renunciated that, in a global business environment, languages continue to have an incredibly important role for the future of any country, necessitating this to be taken into account in the education system.
And indeed, modern languages should actually be considered “international communication skills.”
Independent schools will continue to offer this choice, along with diversity and excellent quality, to young people in Scotland.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is Director of the Secretein Association of Independent Schools
