North South Korea says 'there's no way" to dislodge unilaterally without trust
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - North Korea's foreign minister told the U.N. on Saturday that ongoing sanctions were deepening distrust of the U.S. and there was no way the country was abandoning its nuclear weapons unilaterally under such circumstances.
"Significant measures of goodwill have been taken" over the past year over the past year, we are suspending nuclear and missile tests, the destruction of nuclear testing, and promises not to spread nuclear weapons and technology.
"However, we do not see a corresponding reaction from the United States," he said.
“Without our confidence in the United States, we will not believe in our own national security and under any circumstances there’s no way to disarm unilaterally.”
"While Ri was voicing North South Korea's familiar grievances for Washington's resistance to an "phased-up" approach to abolition of nuclear-weaponity, in which North Korea will be rewarded because it has taken phased measures, its statement seemed significant as it did not dismiss one-way nuclear disarmament as Pyongyang did in the past.
Ri voguised a joint statement from Kim Jong-un and Donald Trump at the first summit between the U.S. president and the North Korean leader in Singapore on June 12, when Kim pledged to work toward "nuclei disarmament on the Korean Peninsula" and Trump gave assurances on North Korea's security.
North Korea is seeking an official end to the 1950–1953 Korean War, but the U.S. says Pyongyang must first give up its nuclear weapons.
Washington has also resisted calls to easify strict international sanctions on North Korea.
"The U.S. is insisting "initially removing nuclear weapons" and increasing pressure through sanctions in order to achieve their goal through coercion, and even objects to "declaration for the end of the war," Ri said.
“Sadponing that sanctions can get us on our knees is a meaningless dream of those who don’t know us.
However, the problem is that the ongoing sanctions exacerbate our disbelief.”
Ri didn't mention whether a second meeting was planned between Kim and Trump, which the U.S. leader underscapitated through the U.N. earlier ahead of the week.
Instead, the minister emphasized on the three meetings between Kim and South Korean leader Moon Jae-in over the past five months and added: "If the country on nuclear disarmament was South Korea rather than the U.S., removing nuclear weapons on the Korean Peninsula wouldn't go to such a stumbling.
However, the tone of Ri's speech was significantly different from the previous year, when he told the U.N. General Assembly that directing North Korean missiles toward the U.S. mainland was inevitable after "eviled" Trump called Kim a "rocket man" on a suicidal mission.
This year at the U.N., Trump, who last year threatened North Korea with "total anninihilation," praised Kim for his courage to take disarmament, but said there's still a lot of work to do and the sanctions should remain in place until North of South Korea eliminates nuclear-weapons.
On Wednesday, Trump underscored that there was no deadline for this and said: "It doesn't matter if it's going to take two years, three years or five months."
China and Russia argue that the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the measures taken.
However, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday that: "Implementation of Security Council Sanctions must necessarily go on until a complete, definitive and confirmed nuclear disarmament takes place."
The Security Council has unanimously stepped up Sanctions on North Korea since 2006 in an attempt to stop funding Pyongyang's non-trival and ballistic missile programs.
Pompeo met with Ri on the sidelines of the U.N. General Assembly and then said he would revisit Pyongyang next month to prepare a second summit.
Pompeo has visited North Korea three times this year, but his last trip went well.
He left Pyongyang in July, saying that progress had been made, but after just a few hours North Korea accusing him of making "gangster demands."
At a meeting with Moon this month, North South Korea vowed to destroy a missile object and a nuclear complex if the U.S. takes "relevant measures."
According to him, Kim has told him that the "relevant measures" he aspires to are the security guarantees that Trump has promised in Singapore and that he wishes to achieve a normalization of relations with Washington.
Harvard Students on a Nugee Break-Friend Course
A new course at Harvard University this year causes all students to sleep more in an attempt to combat a growing "every-half-night" learning culture using caffeine.
Lecturer found that students at the world's number one university often don't even think twice when it's about the very foundations of how to take care of themselves.
Charles Kayssler, Professor of Sleep Medicine at the Harvard Medical School and specialist at Brigham Hospital and Women's Hospital, drew up the course he believed to be the first of its kind in the U.S.
Inspired to start the course, having talked about the impact of sleep deprivation on learning.
“Finally a girl came up to me and said, ‘Why am they only telling this to me in my last year?
“She said that no one has ever talked to her about the importance of a dream, which surprised me,” he told The Telegraph.
The course, which takes place for the first time this year, explains to the students the most essential of how good sleeping habits help academic and athletic achievements, as well as improve overall well-being.
Paul Bahreira, professor of psychiatry at the Harvard Medical Faculty and executive director of the university's health services, said the university had decided to introduce the course after students were found to be suffering from a serious lack of sleep during the week.
The one-hour course involves a series of interactive tasks.
There's an image of a dormitory's room in one of the rooms, at which students click on cups of coffee, cursors, sneakers and books to explain to them the effects of caffeine and light, how athletic performance is affected by a lack of sleep, as well as the importance of the bed-in-time routine.
In another part of the participants it was told how long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risks of heart attack, stroke, depression and cancer.
Then a campus map with interactive icons encourages participants to think about their day-to-day routine.
“We don’t immediately know that it won’t immediately change student behavior.
“But we believe they have the right to know – just as you have a right to know the health effects of choosing to smoke.”
Pride culture of "learning all the night" still exists, he says, adding that modern technology and ever-increasing pressure on students mean sleep deprivation is a growing problem.
Ensuring sufficient and good quality sleep should be the "secret weapon" of students in combating stress, exhaustion and anxiety, he says - even with the aim of avoiding gaining weight, as deprivation of sleep puts the brain starvation, causing them to be constantly starvated.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old student from California who studied chemical and physical biology, helped Professor Kayssler run the course by taking one of his classes in his first year at Harvard.
“He said his course “duced his eyes” and inspired him to spread it all over the campus.
It is hoped that the next step is for all graduate students to be asked to complete a similar syllaboratory program before joining a competitive institution.
Professor Kayssler recommends that students ponder whether to set an alarm for going to go to bed and to wake and to know the harmful effects of "blue light" emanating from the electronic screens and LED lighting, which can disrupt circulatory rhythm and lead to problems with falling asleep.
