Yearbook: Geiger counter inventor
A page of our Sunday Morning year: September 30, 1882, 136 years ago and Broen... the day on which the future physicist Johan Wilhelm “Hans” Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity, an invention that eventually led to the creation of the device, known as a Geiger counter.
A crazy stone in science ever since, the Geiger's counter also became a cornerstone of pop culture, when Coronado's Beamobile was released in 1950, which featured the seemingly impauising scientists Roy Rogers and Dale Evance.
Man: "What in God's name is this
Rogers: It's a Geiger counter, and it's being used to detect radioactive minerals, such as erranium.
“When you put on these headphones, you can actually hear the effects of the atoms emitted by radioactivity in the minerals.”
Evans: "Cerviously care right now!"
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, just a few days after his 63rd birthday.
But the discovery, which bears his name, continues to live on.
