What is a Global Citizen festival and does it help to reduce poverty at all?
This Saturday New York will be hosting the Global Citizen Festival, an annual music event that has an impressive line-up of performers and fulfills an equally impressive mission: eradicating poverty around the world.
“Now, in its seventh year, the Global Civety Festival will gather tens tens of thousands of people at Central Park’s big lawn who will not only enjoy the performances of Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendez, but they’ll also get acquainted with the true purpose of the event – eliminating extreme poverty by 2030.
"Global Citizen", which was launched in 2012, is a follow-up to the "Global Poverty" project, an international advocacy group that hopes to eradicate poverty by increasing the number of people who are actively combating it.
In order to receive a free ticket to the event (unless you wanted to pay for a VIP ticket), visitors had to complete a number of tasks or "actions" such as voluntary participation, sending not an e-mail to a worldwide leader, a phone call, or otherwise connecting in order to help raise awareness about their goal of eradicating the Poverty.
But just how successful is a Global Citizen 12 years before it achieves its goal?
Is the idea of rewarding people with a free-study an ingenious way for them to be persuaded to require an action or is it just another case of so-called "clictivism" - do people think they're doing something significant when signing an online petition or sending a Twitter post?
"Global citizen since 2011" has recorded more than 19 million "actions" by its supporters, who have been insisting on multiple different goals.
It says these actions have helped world leaders declare commitments and policies equivalent to more than $37 billion, which will affect the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
In early 2018, the group cites 390 engagements and announcements stemming from its actions, having already rebursed or raised at least $10 billion.
The group believes that the funds provided have already directly affected nearly 649 million people around the world.
Some of the main commitments include the "Strength of Nutrition," a U.K.-established partnership between investors and contractors, committing to "assisting children to develop their full potential," pledging to provide Rwanda $35 million to help terminating malnutrition in the country after receiving more than 4,700 Twitter posts from "Global citizens."
"With the support of the U.K. government, donors, national governments and global citizens like you, we can turn the social injustice of malnutrition simply into a historic note," Power Ambassador Tracy Ulman told audiences at a live concert in London in April 2018.
The group also said that once more than 5,000 actions were taken urging the UK to improve maternal and children's nutrition, the government had reported funding for a "Emultilance of Effortuation," which would reach 5 million women and children.
Responding to an FAQ on its website asking, “Why do you think we can stop extreme poverty?”
"Global citizen" replied: "It's going to be a long and difficult road - sometimes we're going to fail and we're going to fail again."
But just as with the great movement for civil rights and against apartheid before us we will succeed because we are stronger together.
Janet Jacson, The Weeknd, Shawn Mendez, Cardi B, Giannell Mona are just some performers at this year's event in New York, with Deborah-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
