Image caption World Wide Web inventor Plans to Launch New Internet to Attack Google and Facebook
World Wide Web inventor Tim Berners-Lee is launching an undertaking that aims to rival Facebook, Amazon and Google.
Technology legend's latest project, Inrupt, it's Berners-Lee's open-community platform that builds Berners-Lee Solid's open-community platform.
Solid allows users to choose where their data is stored and which people have permission to access certain information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee poised that the intent behind Inrupt was "alworld domination."
"We have to do it now," he said in terms of the undertaking.
"It's a historic moment."
The application uses Solid technology, which allows people to create their own "online repository of personal data" or POD.
It contains lists, to-draft lists, a calendar, a music library and other personal and professional instruments.
It’s like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify, which are available through every browser at the same time.
What’s unique in the online repository of personal data is that the user determines who has access to what information.
The company calls it "manufacturing powers for personal data."
Inrupt's idea, according to the company's CEO John Bruce, is for the company to put in resources, processes, and relevant skills to help make Solid accessible to everyone.
The company currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform bought by IBM, several developers who have contracted the project, and a group of volunteer programmers.
As of this week, technology developers around the world can create their own decentralized apps using the tools available on Inrupt's website.
Berners-Lee said he and his team didn't talk to Facebook and Google about whether or not to make a complete overhaul when all of their business models renovate completely overnight.
“We don’t want permission from them.”
In a Medium publication on Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that Inrupt's mission was to provide energy for trade and an ecosystem to help protect the integrity and quality of the new network built on Solid's base.
In 1994, Berners-Lee transformed the internet when he set up the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
Berners-Lee has wielded strong clorption in the network's neutrality debate.
Despite launching Inrupt, Berners-Lee will also remain the Founder of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm extremely optimistic about this new era on the internet," Berners-Lee added.
