Germany again postpones launch of new warship
BERLIN (Reuters) - Germany's navy's latest frigate had to be commissioned in 2014 to commute out-of-the-art Cold War-era warships, but it won't take place until at least next year due to malambiguising costs, local media reported.
Commit to commissioning "Rheinland-Pfalz," the brand's flagship new Baden-Wuerttemberg-class fries, has been postponed until the first-half of 2019, according to Die Zeit newspaper citing an army spokeswoman.
The vessel was due to join the Navy in 2014, but the problems that arose after its delivery have affected the fate of the ambitious project.
The Four Baden-Wuerttemberg-class ships, commissioned by the navy in 2007, will replace the outddated Bremen-class frigates.
It's understandable they'll feature a powerful cannon, a set of anti-aircraft and anti-nasal missiles, as well as some shalt technologies, e.g. reduced radar, infrared and acoustic signals.
Other important features include longer periods of maintenance – the latest frigates must be able to be used for up to two years prior to returning to dortake ports.
Continuous delays, however, mean cutting-edge warships - rumoured to allow Germany to demonstrate power across the ocean - would already be old when they enter service, Die Zeit noted.
Mishaled F125 frigate made headlines last year when the German navy officially refused to put the vessel into service and returned it to Blohm and Voss in Hamburg.
It's the first time the navy has returned a ship to a shipbuilder since delivery.
There is little known about the reasons for the return, but German media cited a number of substantial "software and hardware defects" that make the warship unusable if it was sent on a combat mission.
Software flaws are particularly important, as Baden-Wuerttemberg-class vessels will be operated by a crew of about 120 sailors – only half of the crew of the Bremen-class older frigate.
In addition, it turned out that the ship was significantly overwhelmed, which reduced its effectiveness and limited the ability of the Navy to add future upgrades.
The 7,000-tone "Rheinland-Pfalz" is believed to be twice as heavy as the ships of a similar class used by the Germans in World War II.
Apart from faultworthy hardware, the cost of the entire project – including crew training — is also becoming a problem.
It is estimated to have reached a staggering 3.1-billion-euro ($3.6-billion - from the original 2.2 billion euros.
Troubles involving the latest frigates are becoming particularly important in the light of recent warnings that Germany's navy is shrinking.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Barthels, head of the German parliament's defence committee, admitted that the navy was actually "putten by ships capable of carrying out missions."
The official said that the problem has become greater in time, as the old ships are decommissioned, but no new ones have been provided.
He lamented that no Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigate couldn't join the navy.
The National Trust Trasses the Secret Life of Bats
A new study carried out at a mansion in the Scotch Mountains aims to establish how bats use the scenery when they're staggering.
It is hope that the findings will shed new light on the behavior of unique flying mammals and will help in directing future conservation actions.
A study by scientists at Scotland's National Trust will follow the common batons, as well as the brown astraw water-daverages at the Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special recording devices will be placed at key venues on the mansion to track the activities of bats during the season.
NHS employees and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using manual detectors.
Experimate sound analysis of all the recordings will determine the frequency of the calling sounds of the bats, as well as which species make them.
A habitat map and a report will then be compiled in order to create a detailed landscape picture of their behaviour.
Rob Duar, Nature Conservation Advocator for the NTS, hopes the results will undevelop which areas of the habitat are most important for bats and how they are used by any species.
This information will help to determine the benefits of habitat management work, e.g. creating meadows, as well as the best way to maintain forest areas for bats and other related species.
Bottle populations in Scotland and in the UK have generally declined significantly over the last century.
They are threatened by the construction activities that affect their shelters and lead to the loss of habitats.
The risk to the populations may be from wind turbines and lighting, aerial vehicles and some chemical treatments of building materials, as well as attacks by domestic cats.
In reality, bats are not blind.
However, due to their hunting habits at night, their ears are more useful than the eyes when it comes to captivating prey.
They are using an advanced echolocation technique to detect insects and hurdles on their flying trajectory.
NTS, which is responsible for caring for more than 270 Historic Buildings, 38 Ceremonial Gardens and 76,000 hectares of land in the country, takes bats very seriously.
The Trust has trained pundits conducting regular surveys, shelter inspections and occasional rescue operations.
The organisation has even set up Scotland's first and the only special bat reserve at the Threave mansion in Dumfries and Gallwei district, which is home to eight of the ten bat species in Scotland.
Manor manager David Thompson says it's ideal territory for them.
“Here at Threave, we have a great terrain for the bats,” he said.
"We've got old buildings, a lot of grown-up trees and an accomplished suitable habitat.
“But there’s still a lot more for bats that aren’t known, so the work we’re doing here and in other properties will help us better understand what they’ve needed to thrive.”
He stresses that it's important to check for the presence of bats before carrying out property maintenance activities, as it's possible that the unintentional undeterreveillance of one shelter could kill up to 400 female and juvenile bats, as well as destroy an entire local population.
The bats are a protected species and it is illegal to kill, harbour, embarrass or decimate their shelters.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a Scots officer at the Bat Conservation Reveal, has encouraged society to help.
She said: "We still have a lot to learn about our bats and for many of our animal species as we just don't know what's going on with their populations."
