Brexit: UK 'would feel sorry forever' of losing car makers
LONDON (Reuters) - Britain "worry forever" if it loses its status as a world leader in post-Brexit car production, secretary of business Greg Clark said.
He added it was "worrying" that Toyota UK had told the BBC that if the U.K. left the EU without a no-deal, it would temporarily halt production at its factory in Burnaston, near Derby.
“We’re in need of a deal,” he said.
Japan's car maker said the impact of delaying the border in the event of a no-deal Brexit could lead to job losses.
At Bournastone’s Bournaston plant – where Auris and Avensis vehicles are being manufactured – nearly 150,000 cars were produced last year, 90% of which were exported to the rest of the European Union countries.
"My opinion is that if the UK leaves the EU at the end of March, production at our factory will stop," said Marvin Kuk, Toyota's director at Bournaston.
