Here are some basic facts about Palu and Dongala, which are located on the island of Sulawesi:
Pallou is the capital of the province of central Sulawesi, located at the end of a narrow beach on the west coast of Sulawesi, with a population of 3,9800 in 2017.
The city was celebrating its 40th anniversary when the quarrequake and a tsunami hit it.
Dongala is a regency that extends more than 300 km (180 miles) along the coastline of Sulawesi Island.
Regency, which is an administrative region smaller than a province, has an estimated population of 299,200 in 2017.
Fishing and farming are the foundations of Central Sulawesi province's economy, particularly in the Donagala's coastal area.
Nickel's mining is also relevant to the province, but is concentrated mainly in Moovalli, on Sulawesi's vaulty coast.
Palu and Dongalah have been hit several times by tsunami over the past 100 years, according to data from the Indonesian Disaster Reconstruction Agency.
In 1938 the tsunami killed more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of buildings in Dongalah.
Tsunami battered the western part of Dongala and in 1996, when nine people died.
Indonesia sits on the seismic "Piosky Ring of Fire" and is regularly decimated by quakes.
Here's some of the big earthquakes and tsunami in recent years:
2004: Strong tremor on Dec. 26 on the west coast of Indonesia's Aceh province in North Sumatra activates a tsunami wave that hit 14 countries and decimated 226,000 people across the Indian Ocean coast, with more than half of them in Acech.
2005: A series of powerful quakes hit the west coast of Sumatra in late-March and early April.
Thirteens have died on Nyas Island off the coast of Sumatra.
2006: A 6.8-magnitude trestrous tweaked Indonesia's most oolded-holy island, Java by activating a tsunami that shattered in the southern coast, decimating nearly 700 people.
2009: A 7.6-magnitude quake strikes near the city of Padang, West Sumatra province.
More than 1,100 people died.
2010: A 7.5-magnitude earthquake decimated one of Mentitawa islands and activates a 10-metre tsunami that destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: Plithko quarry struck the reagent Piddy Jaya in Aceh, causing destruction and panic as people remembered the ravages of the 2004 deadly quarry and tsunami.
A tsunami was not activated this time, but more than 100 people were killed by collapsed buildings.
2018: Strong tremors decimated the tourist island of Lombok in Indonesia, killing more than 500 people, most of them on the northern side of the island.
The quake dismantled thousands of buildings and left thousands of tourists temporarily in limbo.
