How the U.S.-China trade war could serve Beijing
The first shots in the U.S.-Chinese trade war were deafening and, while the battle is just gaining momentum, a rift between the two nations would benefit Beijing in the long run, experts said.
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - Donald Trump, the U.S. president, has launched its first volley this year, levying key elements of China's exports, including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
The biggest escalation of tensions emerged this week with the new tariffs, which affect $200bn ($150b billion) with an effective taxation of half of all goods imported into the U.S. from China.
Each time Beijing retaliated with counter-new tariffs with five to 10 per cent on $60 Billion U.S. merchandise.
China has vowed to reciprocate any U.S. strike, and the world's second-largest economy is not expected to ignite that anytime soon.
To get Washington to give up is to succumb to the demands, but bowing its head publicly to Washington would be too embarrassing for Xi Jinping, the president of China.
Still, according to experts, if Beijing plays its cards right, the pressure from the trade war with the U.S. could help in the long-haul of China by reducing the inter-reliance of the two economies.
"The fact that a hasty political solution in both Washington and Beijing could create conditions for an economic crunch in one of the two countries is in fact more dangerous than observers acknowledged before," Abigail Grays, an Asia-related research aide at the Center for a New U.S. Center for a New U.S. Security, said in a statement.
Syria 'ready' for refugee returns, Foreign minister says
Syria has announced that it is ready for the voluntary return of refugees and is begging for assistance in re-establishing the country devastated by the war that has lasted more than seven years.
In an address to the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Mualem pointed out that conditions in the state are improving.
"Today the situation on the ground is more stable and safe, thanks to the progress we have in the fight against terrorism," he said.
The government continues to rebuild areas devastated by terrorists in order to bring them back to normal.
Now there are all the conditions for the voluntary return of refugees to the state, which they were forced to leave because of terrorism and the unilateral economic measures that influenced their daily lives and their livelihood.
The U.N. estimates more than 5.5 million Syrians have left the country since the war began in 2011.
Another six million people who still live there need humanitarian assistance.
Al-Moleme said the Syrian regime would welcome any assistance in rebuilding the devastated state.
But he emphasized that conditional assistance or assistance will not be accepted from countries that have financed the insurgencies.
